9
00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:23.964
The grocery app's home screen.

10
00:00:24.244 --> 00:00:37.193
It shows Buy again, Frequently bought together, Picked for you and New arrivals carousels, personalised per customer, restricted to what their dark store stocks, and assembled in under 150 ms.

11
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:50.359
This is the design a news feed or recommendation system interview question in its most common modern shape: a multi-stage pipeline of candidate generation, feature fetch, ranking and re-ranking.

12
00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:55.652
The chapter focuses on the system and its failure modes, not on model architecture.

13
00:00:55.932 --> 00:00:57.937
L1: candidate generation.

14
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:00.607
For 20,000 customers' next basket:

15
00:01:01.957 --> 00:01:07.311
Best-sellers reached 22% recall@20, with the same 20 products for everyone.

16
00:01:07.591 --> 00:01:13.309
Buy-again from the customer's own history reached 66%, but found 0 new items.

17
00:01:13.589 --> 00:01:21.503
Item-to-item co-purchase found 5,411 new items across 2,889 products.

18
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:31.063
A 12 or 5 or 3 blend gave up ~6 points of recall (59%) to find 3,521 new items.

19
00:01:31.343 --> 00:01:33.967
L2: learning to rank, and position bias.

20
00:01:34.247 --> 00:01:39.133
The ranker was trained on 4M impressions logged by the old popularity ranker.

21
00:01:39.413 --> 00:01:46.858
The naive model learned popularity 5x too strongly (1.63 versus a true 0.30).

22
00:01:48.208 --> 00:02:01.861
Adding position as a feature, or inverse-propensity weighting, recovered 0.25 or 0.29, and lifted NDCG@10 from 0.939 to 0.999.

23
00:02:02.141 --> 00:02:04.872
The old ranker scored 0.537.

24
00:02:05.152 --> 00:02:07.947
L3: the feature-fetch fan-out (real Redis).

25
00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:10.851
Fetching features for 400 candidates:

26
00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:19.429
One GET per candidate took 30.9 ms p50 on localhost, about 430 ms with cross-zone round trips.

27
00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:23.699
One MGET plus one HMGET took 0.61 ms.

28
00:02:23.979 --> 00:02:31.744
Caching item features in process for 30 s saved one more trip, but 5.0% of stock flags were stale.

29
00:02:33.094 --> 00:02:35.569
L4: feedback loops and cold start.

30
00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:38.238
Over 30 days with 40 new launches:

31
00:02:38.518 --> 00:02:47.585
A greedy ranker reached 57% of the oracle's clicks and gave 0 of 40 launches a fair trial, though 9 of them beat everything on the shelf.

32
00:02:47.865 --> 00:02:53.091
Thompson sampling reached 92.5% and ranked the best new product 1.

33
00:02:53.371 --> 00:02:59.067
A reserved new-arrivals slot did as well here, but only because the best products happened to be new.

34
00:02:59.347 --> 00:03:00.329
Reading time:

35
00:03:00.609 --> 00:03:02.230
100 min + code.

36
00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:04.433
Part 1.

37
00:03:04.713 --> 00:03:06.633
Introduction and Why It Matters.

38
00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:10.337
A story: the home screen that stopped learning.

39
00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:17.294
The grocery app's first home screen was a merchandising page: hand-picked banners and Best-sellers in your city.

40
00:03:17.574 --> 00:03:22.758
It was replaced by a personalised feed built on a click-through model, and conversion rose.

41
00:03:23.038 --> 00:03:25.641
Six months later, it had quietly degraded:

42
00:03:25.921 --> 00:03:28.289
It only showed what customers already bought.

43
00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:33.475
Buy-again dominated, repeat revenue was steady, and basket size stopped growing.

44
00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:35.334
No one discovered anything.

45
00:03:36.684 --> 00:03:38.241
It believed its own bias.

46
00:03:38.521 --> 00:03:41.721
The model was retrained nightly on clicks from its own feed.

47
00:03:42.001 --> 00:03:47.313
Items at the top were clicked more because they were at the top, so the model ranked them even higher.

48
00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:49.257
New products never took off.

49
00:03:49.537 --> 00:03:59.607
A new regional snack brand, better liked than anything in its category, received almost no impressions: it had no history, so it scored low, so it got no history.

50
00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:01.529
Out-of-stock items appeared.

51
00:04:01.809 --> 00:04:03.729
Features came from a nightly batch.

52
00:04:04.009 --> 00:04:08.169
Items that sold out at 10 am were still on the feed at 7 pm.

53
00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:10.586
It got slow.

54
00:04:10.866 --> 00:04:14.258
Each feed request made hundreds of calls to the feature store.

55
00:04:14.538 --> 00:04:20.405
The p99 crept to 900 ms, and the home screen showed a skeleton for a second.

56
00:04:20.685 --> 00:04:25.421
A recommendation system is a closed loop: what it shows determines what it learns.

57
00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:30.927
This chapter designs the pipeline and, just as important, the controls around the loop.

58
00:04:32.277 --> 00:04:32.960
Quote:

59
00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:34.904
The thesis of this chapter.

60
00:04:35.184 --> 00:04:40.240
A feed is a multi-stage retrieval-and-ranking pipeline wrapped in a feedback loop.

61
00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:49.245
Retrieval decides what's possible: blend sources with explicit quotas, because each one sees a different slice of what customers want (L1).

62
00:04:49.525 --> 00:04:55.669
Ranking learns from logs its predecessor shaped, so correct for position and selection bias (L2).

63
00:04:55.949 --> 00:05:03.651
Serving is a fan-out problem: batch feature reads and cache wisely, but never cache what must be fresh, like stock (L3).

64
00:05:03.931 --> 00:05:11.653
And the loop must be kept open with deliberate exploration, or the system stops learning about anything it doesn't already show (L4).

65
00:05:13.003 --> 00:05:15.307
