558
00:38:27.804 --> 00:38:28.743
Part 6.

559
00:38:29.023 --> 00:38:30.303
Production Concerns.

560
00:38:31.553 --> 00:38:32.705
Failure modes.

561
00:38:32.985 --> 00:38:34.329
Here is a quick table:

562
00:38:34.609 --> 00:38:36.891
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

563
00:38:37.171 --> 00:38:38.643
One dominant retriever.

564
00:38:38.923 --> 00:38:39.734
Symptom:

565
00:38:40.014 --> 00:38:41.699
No discovery (L1).

566
00:38:41.979 --> 00:38:42.982
Mitigation:

567
00:38:43.262 --> 00:38:45.033
Quotas, multiple sources.

568
00:38:45.313 --> 00:38:46.785
Training on biased logs.

569
00:38:47.065 --> 00:38:47.875
Symptom:

570
00:38:48.155 --> 00:38:50.139
Rich-get-richer ranking (L2).

571
00:38:50.419 --> 00:38:51.422
Mitigation:

572
00:38:51.702 --> 00:38:54.539
Position features, propensities, exploration.

573
00:38:54.819 --> 00:38:56.483
Per-candidate feature calls.

574
00:38:56.763 --> 00:38:57.574
Symptom:

575
00:38:57.854 --> 00:38:59.603
Latency blow-up (L3).

576
00:38:59.883 --> 00:39:00.886
Mitigation:

577
00:39:01.166 --> 00:39:03.043
Batched reads, feature services.

578
00:39:03.323 --> 00:39:04.326
Cached stock.

579
00:39:04.606 --> 00:39:05.417
Symptom:

580
00:39:05.697 --> 00:39:07.723
Out-of-stock items shown (L3).

581
00:39:08.003 --> 00:39:09.006
Mitigation:

582
00:39:09.286 --> 00:39:10.651
Live stock filter.

583
00:39:10.931 --> 00:39:12.126
No exploration.

584
00:39:12.406 --> 00:39:13.217
Symptom:

585
00:39:13.497 --> 00:39:15.587
Cold items never shown (L4).

586
00:39:15.867 --> 00:39:16.870
Mitigation:

587
00:39:17.150 --> 00:39:19.603
Thompson slots, new-arrivals carousel.

588
00:39:19.883 --> 00:39:20.886
Ranker outage.

589
00:39:21.166 --> 00:39:21.977
Symptom:

590
00:39:22.257 --> 00:39:23.473
Empty home screen.

591
00:39:23.753 --> 00:39:24.755
Mitigation:

592
00:39:25.035 --> 00:39:26.443
Fallback feed, breaker.

593
00:39:26.723 --> 00:39:28.174
Training or serving skew.

594
00:39:28.454 --> 00:39:29.265
Symptom:

595
00:39:29.545 --> 00:39:31.443
Offline gains vanish online.

596
00:39:31.723 --> 00:39:32.726
Mitigation:

597
00:39:33.006 --> 00:39:35.225
Feature store, point-in-time joins.

598
00:39:36.575 --> 00:39:37.791
Metric myopia.

599
00:39:38.071 --> 00:39:38.881
Symptom:

600
00:39:39.161 --> 00:39:40.676
Clicks up, baskets down.

601
00:39:40.956 --> 00:39:41.959
Mitigation:

602
00:39:42.239 --> 00:39:44.308
Purchase-based labels, guardrails.

603
00:39:45.658 --> 00:39:46.746
Observability.

604
00:39:47.026 --> 00:39:50.333
Per-stage latency and candidate counts; fallback rate.

605
00:39:50.613 --> 00:39:55.519
Impressions by retriever source and exploration flag; out-of-stock impression rate.

606
00:39:55.799 --> 00:39:59.533
Model score distributions and feature freshness; drift alerts.

607
00:39:59.813 --> 00:40:01.541
Online metrics per carousel:

608
00:40:01.821 --> 00:40:05.191
CTR, add-to-cart, purchase, new-product adoption.

609
00:40:06.541 --> 00:40:08.099
Security and privacy.

610
00:40:08.379 --> 00:40:18.811
Recommendations reveal purchase history: never show sensitive categories (health, intimate care) in shareable surfaces; respect opt-outs of personalisation.

611
00:40:19.091 --> 00:40:24.403
Aggregate thresholds for customers also bought, so individual behaviour isn't exposed.

612
00:40:24.683 --> 00:40:29.483
Data minimisation and retention for behavioural logs (Ch 36).

613
00:40:30.833 --> 00:40:31.729
Performance.

614
00:40:32.009 --> 00:40:38.622
Batch feature reads (L3); keep the ranker co-located or use a fast RPC with a tight timeout.

615
00:40:38.902 --> 00:40:42.977
Score only what survived filtering; cap candidates per carousel.

616
00:40:43.257 --> 00:40:47.779
Cache the non-personal parts (city or store popular lists) aggressively.

617
00:40:49.129 --> 00:40:49.855
Cost.

618
00:40:50.135 --> 00:40:54.892
Precompute candidates only for active users; compute per request for the rest.

619
00:40:55.172 --> 00:41:00.143
Model serving cost scales with candidates times requests: trim candidates early.

620
00:41:00.423 --> 00:41:05.543
Exploration has an opportunity cost; budget it explicitly (Ch 35).

621
00:41:06.893 --> 00:41:07.831
Part 7.

622
00:41:08.111 --> 00:41:09.818
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

623
00:41:11.068 --> 00:41:11.985
Watch out:

624
00:41:12.265 --> 00:41:12.905
1.

625
00:41:13.185 --> 00:41:14.252
One retriever.

626
00:41:14.532 --> 00:41:17.455
Buy-again alone found 0 new items (L1).

627
00:41:18.805 --> 00:41:19.722
Watch out:

628
00:41:20.002 --> 00:41:20.706
2.

629
00:41:20.986 --> 00:41:23.738
Evaluating rankers without measuring retrieval.

630
00:41:24.018 --> 00:41:26.535
The ranker can't recover what retrieval missed.

631
00:41:27.885 --> 00:41:28.803
Watch out:

632
00:41:29.083 --> 00:41:29.829
3.

633
00:41:30.109 --> 00:41:31.880
Training on clicks without position.

634
00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:37.109
The model learns its predecessor's bias (L2: popularity 5x too strong).

635
00:41:38.459 --> 00:41:39.377
Watch out:

636
00:41:39.657 --> 00:41:40.361
4.

637
00:41:40.641 --> 00:41:42.070
No propensity logging.

638
00:41:42.350 --> 00:41:45.571
You can't debias or evaluate counterfactually later.

639
00:41:46.921 --> 00:41:47.839
Watch out:

640
00:41:48.119 --> 00:41:48.887
5.

641
00:41:49.167 --> 00:41:50.916
Per-candidate feature fetches.

642
00:41:51.196 --> 00:41:53.799
800 round trips per request (L3).

643
00:41:55.149 --> 00:41:56.066
Watch out:

644
00:41:56.346 --> 00:41:57.093
6.

645
00:41:57.373 --> 00:41:58.461
Caching stock.

646
00:41:58.741 --> 00:42:01.706
5% stale flags in 30 s (L3).

647
00:42:03.056 --> 00:42:03.973
Watch out:

648
00:42:04.253 --> 00:42:05.000
7.

649
00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:06.560
Pure exploitation.

650
00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:09.251
New products never get a chance (L4:

651
00:42:09.531 --> 00:42:10.789
0 or 40).

652
00:42:12.139 --> 00:42:13.057
Watch out:

653
00:42:13.337 --> 00:42:14.019
8.

654
00:42:14.299 --> 00:42:15.878
Optimising clicks only.

655
00:42:16.158 --> 00:42:18.249
Clickbait items, smaller baskets.

656
00:42:19.599 --> 00:42:20.516
Watch out:

657
00:42:20.796 --> 00:42:21.543
9.

658
00:42:21.823 --> 00:42:22.804
No fallback.

659
00:42:23.084 --> 00:42:25.708
A ranker outage becomes an empty home screen.

660
00:42:27.058 --> 00:42:27.975
Watch out:

661
00:42:28.255 --> 00:42:28.959
10.

662
00:42:29.239 --> 00:42:31.031
Precomputing everything nightly.

663
00:42:31.311 --> 00:42:34.362
Stale for active users, wasted for inactive ones.

664
00:42:35.712 --> 00:42:36.629
Watch out:

665
00:42:36.909 --> 00:42:37.741
11.

666
00:42:38.021 --> 00:42:39.557
No diversity controls.

667
00:42:39.837 --> 00:42:41.907
Ten kinds of milk in one carousel.

668
00:42:43.257 --> 00:42:44.174
Watch out:

669
00:42:44.454 --> 00:42:45.243
12.

670
00:42:45.523 --> 00:42:48.041
Offline wins shipped without A or B tests.

671
00:42:48.321 --> 00:42:50.369
Logged data shares the loop's bias.

672
00:42:51.719 --> 00:42:52.593
Part 8.

673
00:42:52.873 --> 00:42:54.217
Interview Q and A.

674
00:42:55.467 --> 00:42:56.299
Beginner.

675
00:42:56.579 --> 00:42:57.603
Question 1.

676
00:42:57.883 --> 00:43:00.977
Design a recommendation feed, what are the stages?

677
00:43:01.257 --> 00:43:02.622
Thirty-second answer:

678
00:43:02.902 --> 00:43:20.673
Retrieve a few hundred candidates from several cheap sources (history, co-purchase, popular, embeddings); filter by availability and eligibility; fetch features; rank with a learned model; re-rank for diversity, business rules and exploration; render with impression logging.

679
00:43:20.953 --> 00:43:26.670
Offline pipelines build candidates, features and models; streaming keeps fast features fresh.

680
00:43:28.020 --> 00:43:29.023
Question 2.

681
00:43:29.303 --> 00:43:32.183
What candidate sources would you use for grocery?

682
00:43:32.463 --> 00:43:33.828
Thirty-second answer:

683
00:43:34.108 --> 00:43:47.271
Buy-again (history with due-ness), item-to-item co-purchase from the cart and recent orders, popular and seasonal by city or store, embedding similarity, and new arrivals, blended with explicit quotas.

684
00:43:48.621 --> 00:43:49.666
Question 3.

685
00:43:49.946 --> 00:43:52.378
Precompute feeds or compute on request?

686
00:43:52.658 --> 00:43:54.023
Thirty-second answer:

687
00:43:54.303 --> 00:43:55.114
Hybrid.

688
00:43:55.394 --> 00:44:04.695
Precompute slow parts (candidate lists, co-purchase neighbours) for active users, and do filtering, fresh features, ranking and rules on request.

689
00:44:04.975 --> 00:44:10.543
Pure precompute is stale for stock and recent purchases; pure on-request is expensive.

690
00:44:11.893 --> 00:44:12.960
Question 4.

691
00:44:13.240 --> 00:44:14.584
What is a feature store?

692
00:44:14.864 --> 00:44:16.229
Thirty-second answer:

693
00:44:16.509 --> 00:44:28.563
A system that defines features once and serves them consistently offline (point-in-time correct training data) and online (low-latency lookups), so training and serving use the same values.

694
00:44:28.843 --> 00:44:34.005
Online stores are typically Redis or DynamoDB; offline stores are the lake.

695
00:44:35.355 --> 00:44:36.358
Intermediate.

696
00:44:36.638 --> 00:44:37.747
Question 5.

697
00:44:38.027 --> 00:44:40.779
How do you keep feature fetch within the latency budget?

698
00:44:41.059 --> 00:44:42.425
Thirty-second answer:

699
00:44:42.705 --> 00:44:59.473
Batch every read a request needs into as few calls as possible (MGET or HMGET, pipelining, a feature service that fans in), co-locate the store, cache slow-changing item features in process, precompute where possible, and never do per-candidate calls.

700
00:45:00.823 --> 00:45:01.932
Question 6.

701
00:45:02.212 --> 00:45:04.985
How do you handle availability in recommendations?

702
00:45:05.265 --> 00:45:06.631
Thirty-second answer:

703
00:45:06.911 --> 00:45:18.089
Filter candidates by live stock at the customer's dark store before ranking, using a stock-change stream into the feature store (seconds of freshness), and re-check the final shown items.

704
00:45:18.369 --> 00:45:21.527
Never cache stock for long; demote low-stock items.

705
00:45:22.877 --> 00:45:23.965
Question 7.

706
00:45:24.245 --> 00:45:25.866
What should the ranker optimise?

707
00:45:26.146 --> 00:45:27.511
Thirty-second answer:

708
00:45:27.791 --> 00:45:35.663
For grocery, add-to-cart and purchase (value-weighted), not just clicks, with guardrails on availability, margin and diversity.

709
00:45:35.943 --> 00:45:42.258
Use a multi-objective score or a model per objective combined with weights tuned by experiments.

710
00:45:43.608 --> 00:45:44.632
Question 8.

711
00:45:44.912 --> 00:45:46.640
How do you ensure diversity?

712
00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:48.285
Thirty-second answer:

713
00:45:48.565 --> 00:46:02.176
Re-rank with caps per category and brand, or maximal marginal relevance (trade relevance against similarity to items already chosen); dedupe across carousels; reserve slots for exploration and new arrivals.

714
00:46:03.526 --> 00:46:04.593
Question 9.

715
00:46:04.873 --> 00:46:06.942
How do you handle a brand-new customer?

716
00:46:07.222 --> 00:46:08.587
Thirty-second answer:

717
00:46:08.867 --> 00:46:24.227
Start with popular and seasonal items for their city and dark store, use signup context (location, stated preferences such as vegetarian), and update quickly after the first sessions and order, streaming features make the second visit personal.

718
00:46:25.577 --> 00:46:26.345
Senior.

719
00:46:26.625 --> 00:46:27.671
Question 10.

720
00:46:27.951 --> 00:46:30.319
Your model is trained on its own feed's clicks.

721
00:46:30.599 --> 00:46:32.583
What goes wrong and how do you fix it?

722
00:46:32.863 --> 00:46:34.228
Thirty-second answer:

723
00:46:34.508 --> 00:46:44.791
Position and selection bias: items shown higher are clicked more because they're higher, and items never shown have no labels, so the model reinforces its predecessor.

724
00:46:45.071 --> 00:46:57.081
Fix by logging position and propensity, modelling position in training (set to a fixed value at serving) or inverse-propensity weighting, and exploring to get labels for unshown items.

725
00:46:58.431 --> 00:46:59.562
Question 11.

726
00:46:59.842 --> 00:47:01.826
How do new products get a fair chance?

727
00:47:02.106 --> 00:47:03.471
Thirty-second answer:

728
00:47:03.751 --> 00:47:05.287
Explicit exploration:

729
00:47:05.567 --> 00:47:21.589
Thompson sampling or UCB slots that explore in proportion to uncertainty, content features to borrow priors from similar items, a new-arrivals carousel for a launch window, and guardrails so exploration doesn't show clearly poor items for long.

730
00:47:22.939 --> 00:47:24.027
Question 12.

731
00:47:24.307 --> 00:47:26.077
How do you evaluate a new ranker?

732
00:47:26.357 --> 00:47:27.723
Thirty-second answer:

733
00:47:28.003 --> 00:47:45.944
Offline on logged data with debiased metrics (NDCG with propensities, counterfactual estimates); then online via A or B tests or interleaving on add-to-cart, basket value, adoption of new products and guardrails; check segments (new versus repeat, cities).

734
00:47:47.294 --> 00:47:48.574
Question 13.

735
00:47:48.854 --> 00:47:50.838
What happens if the ranker is down?

736
00:47:51.118 --> 00:47:52.483
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:47:52.763 --> 00:48:05.414
A timeout and breaker around the ranker; fallback to the user's last good ranking (cached) or a store-level popular list; still apply live stock filtering and business rules; log the degraded mode.

738
00:48:05.694 --> 00:48:07.657
The home screen must never be empty.

739
00:48:09.007 --> 00:48:10.201
Question 14.

740
00:48:10.481 --> 00:48:13.511
How do you build item-to-item recommendations at scale?

741
00:48:13.791 --> 00:48:15.156
Thirty-second answer:

742
00:48:15.436 --> 00:48:29.303
Count co-occurrences in baskets (a batch job over the lake, pruned to pairs above a support threshold), normalise by popularity (cosine, lift or PMI), keep the top-N neighbours per item, and refresh daily.

743
00:48:29.583 --> 00:48:33.807
At request time, aggregate neighbours of the cart and recent purchases.

744
00:48:34.087 --> 00:48:38.353
Embeddings (item2vec, two-tower) generalise to sparse items.

745
00:48:39.703 --> 00:48:40.599
Architect.

746
00:48:40.879 --> 00:48:42.074
Question 15.

747
00:48:42.354 --> 00:48:46.727
Design the home feed for a 300-store grocery platform end to end.

748
00:48:47.007 --> 00:48:48.373
Thirty-second answer:

749
00:48:48.653 --> 00:49:10.925
Event logging with positions and propensities; a lake with daily training and candidate generation; streaming features and stock; an online feature store; a home feed service doing retrieval with quotas, availability filtering, batched feature fetch, ranking, re-ranking with diversity, rules and exploration; fallback feeds; experiments and guardrails.

750
00:49:12.275 --> 00:49:13.469
Question 16.

751
00:49:13.749 --> 00:49:16.523
How do you govern the business side of recommendations?

752
00:49:16.803 --> 00:49:18.168
Thirty-second answer:

753
00:49:18.448 --> 00:49:32.485
Explicit policies in the re-ranker: sponsored slot limits and labelling, category exclusions, diversity caps, pins with expiry, fairness to small brands (exploration budgets), and dashboards for merchandising.

754
00:49:32.765 --> 00:49:35.432
Changes go through experiments with guardrails.

755
00:49:36.782 --> 00:49:38.126
Question 17.

756
00:49:38.406 --> 00:49:42.609
How would you reduce latency further if the budget drops to 50 ms?

757
00:49:42.889 --> 00:49:44.254
Thirty-second answer:

758
00:49:44.534 --> 00:50:01.046
Precompute more (per-user ranked lists refreshed on events), cache store-level components, shrink candidate sets, use a cheaper first-stage ranker and a heavier one only for the top, co-locate model and features, and move exploration decisions to precomputed slots.

759
00:50:02.396 --> 00:50:03.569
Question 18.

760
00:50:03.849 --> 00:50:06.260
What are the privacy risks of recommendations?

761
00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:07.905
Thirty-second answer:

762
00:50:08.185 --> 00:50:20.665
Recommendations reveal behaviour (health products, pregnancy-related purchases) on shared screens and in notifications; customers also bought can leak individual behaviour for rare items.

763
00:50:20.945 --> 00:50:28.327
Mitigate with sensitive-category exclusions, aggregation thresholds, personalisation opt-outs, and retention limits.

764
00:50:29.677 --> 00:50:30.914
Question 19.

765
00:50:31.194 --> 00:50:33.413
Build versus buy for recommendations?

766
00:50:33.693 --> 00:50:35.058
Thirty-second answer:

767
00:50:35.338 --> 00:50:51.061
Buy or adopt platform pieces (feature store, experimentation, model serving, vector search) where mature; build the domain logic (retrievers, availability, business rules, exploration policy) that depends on your inventory and customers.

768
00:50:51.341 --> 00:50:55.757
Managed recommender services are useful for small catalogues and teams.

769
00:50:57.107 --> 00:50:58.216
Question 20.

770
00:50:58.496 --> 00:51:01.696
How do you know the recommender is good for the business long-term?

771
00:51:01.976 --> 00:51:03.341
Thirty-second answer:

772
00:51:03.621 --> 00:51:20.795
Track long-horizon metrics: retention, basket growth, category breadth per customer, new-product adoption, complaint rates; use long-running holdouts (a small group without personalisation or with a baseline); watch for catalogue concentration (Gini of impressions).

773
00:51:22.145 --> 00:51:23.041
Part 9.

774
00:51:23.321 --> 00:51:24.729
Summary and Practice.

775
00:51:25.979 --> 00:51:27.067
Key takeaways.

776
00:51:27.347 --> 00:51:32.467
Multi-stage pipeline: retrieve, then filter, then features, then rank, then re-rank.

777
00:51:32.747 --> 00:51:37.973
Blend retrievers with quotas; buy-again wins recall but finds nothing new (L1).

778
00:51:38.253 --> 00:51:43.245
Your logs are biased by your ranker: model position, log propensities (L2).

779
00:51:43.525 --> 00:51:46.363
Batch feature reads; never cache stock (L3).

780
00:51:46.643 --> 00:51:50.269
Explore deliberately or the loop closes on itself (L4).

781
00:51:50.549 --> 00:51:53.472
Fallbacks, guardrails and experiments keep it safe.

782
00:51:54.822 --> 00:51:56.145
Decision cheat sheet.

783
00:51:56.425 --> 00:51:57.769
Here is a quick table:

784
00:51:58.049 --> 00:51:59.222
Need, and Choice.

785
00:51:59.502 --> 00:52:00.483
Candidates:

786
00:52:00.763 --> 00:52:06.075
Buy-again + item-to-item + popular or seasonal + embeddings, with quotas.

787
00:52:06.355 --> 00:52:07.166
Ranker:

788
00:52:07.446 --> 00:52:12.203
GBDT or deep model on purchase-weighted labels, position-aware training.

789
00:52:12.483 --> 00:52:13.358
Features:

790
00:52:13.638 --> 00:52:17.265
Online store, one batched read per group, stock via events.

791
00:52:17.545 --> 00:52:18.334
Serving:

792
00:52:18.614 --> 00:52:21.878
Hybrid precompute + online ranking; fallback feed.

793
00:52:22.158 --> 00:52:23.246
Exploration:

794
00:52:23.526 --> 00:52:27.238
Thompson slots + new-arrivals window; log propensities.

795
00:52:27.518 --> 00:52:28.478
Re-ranking:

796
00:52:28.758 --> 00:52:32.235
Diversity caps, dedupe, rules, labelled sponsored slots.

797
00:52:33.585 --> 00:52:34.716
Exercises.

798
00:52:34.996 --> 00:52:38.324
(B2) In L1, change the blend to 8 or 8 or 4.

799
00:52:38.604 --> 00:52:40.759
Plot recall against new items found.

800
00:52:41.039 --> 00:52:48.121
(B3) In L2, make examination fall off more steeply (0.8 instead of 0.35).

801
00:52:48.401 --> 00:52:50.599
How much worse does the naive model get?

802
00:52:50.879 --> 00:52:56.169
(B3) In L3, add a stock-change subscription that invalidates the local cache.

803
00:52:56.449 --> 00:52:58.284
What happens to staleness and trips?

804
00:52:58.564 --> 00:53:05.711
(B4) In L4, add position bias to the carousel and use Thompson sampling with position-debiased updates.

805
00:53:05.991 --> 00:53:14.332
(B4) Design the impression log schema with everything needed for L2's debiasing and L4's counterfactual evaluation.

